#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""focus — name a goal, then count 25 minutes down against it.

A pomodoro that insists on the first half of the ritual: you say what the
block is for before the clock starts, and that sentence stays on screen the
whole time. When the countdown ends it rings, and the session — goal, length,
whether you saw it through — is appended to a log you can read back.

    focus                          ask for the goal, then count 25 minutes down
    focus "rewrite the intro"      goal on the command line, same 25 minutes
    focus -m 50 "deep work"        a different length
    focus -t aquarium "read"       pick the scene instead of taking pot luck
    focus --preview airport        watch a theme without starting a block
    focus --log                    the sessions so far, today's total on top
    focus --history                what you did today, block by block
    focus --history yesterday      the same for yesterday, a weekday or a date
    focus --no-finale "quiet"      land the block without the celebration
    focus --me                     set the name and birthday it greets you by
    focus --login                  keep the history in your Bang Labs account
    focus --whoami                 which account this machine logs to

Themes draw a scene behind the clock, a frame every tenth of a second. Each
block takes a random one unless -t or $FOCUS_THEME says otherwise:

    plain      nothing but the goal, the clock and the bar
    aquarium   fish crossing the tank, seaweed swaying on the floor,
               bubbles rising, and once in a while a crab or a squid
    airport    a terminal and tower along the bottom, departures rolling
               out and climbing away, arrivals descending onto the runway,
               helicopters settling on the apron, tugs and clouds
    space      a twinkling starfield past a ringed planet, with rockets,
               satellites and comets going through
    galaxy     a spiral galaxy turning slowly against the field
    desert     dunes under a blazing sun, an oasis, a vulture circling, and
               camel caravans walking the crest
    farm       barn, silo and turning windmill, hens pecking round the
               yard, a cow in the field, a tractor going by
    matrix     glyph rain down every column, bright head, fading tail
    neurons    cells wired into a net, pulses running the dendrites and
               firing the cell at the far end
    haunted    a house on the hill with candles guttering behind its windows,
               bats, a ghost off the roof, lightning
    christmas  snow over a lit tree and a cabin with its fire going, a snowman
               in the yard, and a sleigh crossing every minute or two
    easter     a meadow under a rainbow, bunnies hopping through and leaving
               painted eggs on the grass behind them
    birthday   bunting, balloons, confetti and a cake with candles, with your
               name across the top

The last four keep to their dates. Random offers the haunted house only in the
Monday-to-Sunday week that holds 31 October, Christmas through December, and
Easter from Palm Sunday to Easter Monday — Easter moves, so it is computed.
Your birthday outranks the lot: on the day, random draws nothing else. focus
asks for your name and birthday once, keeps them in ~/focus/profile.json, and
--me changes them. -t names any of these any day of the year.

`-t plain` is the way back to a bare clock; random never picks it, and the
picker walks the lot in the order above, plain included.

While it runs:  space pauses and resumes, q stops early, Ctrl-C does the
same. t opens the scene picker along the bottom: the arrow keys walk the rota
and draw each one behind the clock as you go, and t, enter or escape leaves it
where you stopped. The block keeps counting the whole time.

d, in the picker, keeps the scene you are on as the one every block draws from
now on — it turns green in the strip to say so, and d again drops it and puts
the lottery back. It is remembered in ~/focus/profile.json; $FOCUS_THEME still
wins for a shell that sets it, and -t still wins for one block.

The bottom right corner keeps the GlyphClock reading: the glyph standing for
this 90-minute block of the UTC day, one copy for the first half hour of it,
two for the second, three for the last. The glyph is the reading, so it stands
on its own. It is the same on every machine in the world —
glyphclock.bang-labs.eu.

A block that runs to zero ends on six seconds of celebration — fireworks, a
shockwave or confetti, drawn at random over the theme, with DONE across the
middle. Any key closes it early, --no-finale skips it. Closing it asks what
comes next: f starts a new session, r runs the same goal again, q quits.

Everything lands in ~/focus/sessions.csv, on this machine and nowhere else.
Run --login once and the record moves to your Bang Labs account instead — the
same one every bang-labs.eu site uses — so a block started on the laptop is
there in --history on the desktop. Signed out, focus never touches the
network; --logout goes back to the CSV. A block that cannot be uploaded when
it ends waits in ~/focus/pending.jsonl and goes up with the next one, so
nothing is lost to a dropped connection.

--log is the running tail of the log;
--history reads a single day back — when each block started and stopped, what
it was for, how long you were away between them, and the whole day drawn as
one strip of hours. It takes today, yesterday, a weekday name, a date written
day first (14-9, 14/09/2026, ISO), a bare day of the month, or week for the
last seven days at once.

Nothing outside the standard library is needed; notify-send and paplay are
used for the alarm if they exist.
"""

